{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem","id":495992,"title":"The Armies of Irem","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-28T23:34:03-07:00","id":495992,"title":"The Armies of Irem","content":"What does an Iremite legion look like? Were they more-or-less recognizable when compared against other nation-states of the era, or did they operate with occult technology that would have made them unfamiliar to us? Was the typical Iremite legion accompanied by undead, or Amkhata? Or did they distinguish themselves purely by virtue of superior training and discipline?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T01:48:34-07:00","id":496094,"title":"","content":"If memory serves me here from what was mentioned- there were relics involved, sure, but the big thing was that Irem had bronze age technology before the bronze age was supposed to happen.<br /> <br /> An impressive advantage 6000 years ago, now, not so much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T04:30:56-07:00","id":496180,"title":"","content":"I kind of got the impression that Amkhata were considered blasphemous in the time of Irem. Sothis Ascends, for example, notes that Amkhata are sometimes encountered in vaults and secret places where they were sealed and imprisoned by the forces of Irem. They were a weapon of internal dissenters, and possibly tools of the few rival empires Irem encountered.<br /> <br /> As for Irem, its armies were made of slaves. One of the duties of the Maa-Kep was to shepherd the slave soldiers of Irem into battle. The legions were organised under the ostensible command of Shan'iatu Guildmasters, but the Jackal's Hunger makes it seem like they delegated those responsibilities to the nobility on occasion. Other than that, it is as StSword mentioned. Relic weapons would be wielded by selected individuals, many of the soldiers were outfitted with mundane or even mystic bronze, and the army as a whole was of a size and cohesion that overwhelmed the far smaller communities they encountered with ease. I would wager that in almost all cases, they felt like the superpower of the era.<br /> <br /> There are allusions to more than that, however. Cursed Necropolis D.C. mentions seeing the walls of Irem, guarded by a host of bound ghosts and demons. Sothis Ascends mentions the ghosts of a legion of Iremite warriors, accompanied by a pair of enchained Chthonic Beings. Since the Utterances are, essentially, rote-learned spells of the Shan'iatu, I would say that anything you can create via Utterance is something Irem would have deployed into battle. I would say that, at times, the Shan'iatu were capable of bringing their full sorcery to bear against an enemy. It wasn't really a necessity though until they began to encounter true resistance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T05:17:49-07:00","id":496190,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah, another thing I almost forgot.<br /> <br /> The Dancers were some of Irem's greatest champions, among the only Deathless to have been fighters in their mortal lives. Of the Arisen today, I can only think of Shemsu, Maa-Kep Flail Bearers, and those of the Dancer's disciples who learnt the correct movements to disable or destroy an opponent as examples of individuals who would have been involved in fighting before undergoing the Rite.<br /> <br /> The Dancers would have been particularly fascinating examples, since the Seba they could have carried could have any one of an impossibly vast range of effects. They were the champions of the army, where the Shemsu stayed behind to protect the temples and the Flail Bearers drove the bulk of the forces into battle. I can picture them fighting with blows that strike with the force of a lightning strike, knocking down whole groups of men with a shockwave. Another might wield blades that run with a kind of spectral fire, or move in such a way that they flit in and out of Neter-Khetet as they tear their way through enemy ranks. Each Dancer would have likely been unique, however, and hardly representative of the vast bulk of Irem's armies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T11:31:34-07:00","id":496518,"title":"","content":"They had bronze before anyone else, and magical bronze (pyropus) at that. Add relics, undead, and Shan'iatu Utterances to all that and you get one hell of a fighting force.<br /> <br /> Also; the Su-Menent led the armies against Ki-En-Gir.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T05:03:25-07:00","id":497324,"title":"","content":"That being said, they were also stymied by at least one other superpower at the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T06:26:49-07:00","id":497375,"title":"","content":"My impression of the war was that the armies of Irem were easily more powerful than those of Ki-En-Gir, but that the prophetic powers of that nation's sorcerers made it hard to make any serious gains.<br /> <br /> And to be fair, the moment the Shan'iatu took a personal hand that rival superpower crumbled. Which, if you think about it, is further evidence that the legions were usually left to the command of mortals despite ostensibly falling under the joint command of Shan'iatu Guildmasters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T12:15:17-07:00","id":497714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=497375#post497375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My impression of the war was that the armies of Irem were easily more powerful than those of Ki-En-Gir, but that the prophetic powers of that nation's sorcerers made it hard to make any serious gains.<br /> <br /> And to be fair, the moment the Shan'iatu took a personal hand that rival superpower crumbled. Which, if you think about it, is further evidence that the legions were usually left to the command of mortals despite ostensibly falling under the joint command of Shan'iatu Guildmasters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, though to look further into their armies:<br /> <br /> As for how the actual armies were organized into legions, I believe it would be safe to assume that they were extremely similar to Sumerian/Akkadian legions, such as battalions being made of roughly six hundred or more slaves, perhaps 7 battalions per legion, primarily spearmen with swordsmen interspersed with them, things like that. THEN you take into account the magic 8 ball bullshit the Shan'iatu outfitted their favored slaves with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 04-30-2015, 03:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T12:30:50-07:00","id":497726,"title":"","content":"Great help everybody, thanks. I'm pretty sure the Shan'iatu were also experiencing a form of 'Descent', where by the time of Irem they were diminished to a mere fragment of the former power they claimed. So I believe that the Shan'iatu are going to essentially behave like they are rationing what resources they have left. While Shan'iatu might very well represent to those that serve them that they have infinite power, the truth is that each Utterance diminishes them slightly, and they are aware they will soon sputter out. That is why they become so fascinated with the Rite and performing it at all costs, to escape fading away as the rest of the world did.<br /> <br /> Accordingly, I believe the Iremite armies would be fairly mundane at first glance. Mundane military tactics and advanced strategies require no energy from the Shan'iatu to perform, so this would be the tool they would use to resolve every dispute they could. These armies would be provisioned with Iremite bronze but beyond revolutionary tactics, recognizable. Because I know nothing about actual infantry combat, I've decided they organize themselves into hexagonal formations with multiple facings that rotate to accommodate attack from various enemy forces. One side spear, one side shields, etc. So if you were to observe an Iremite Legion fighting from above, you'd see what look like hexagonal groups of men spinning as the pace and location of the battle changed.<br /> <br /> If those efforts fail, I believe the Shan'iatu would use magic without much hesitation. Part of the whole gambit of convincing people you have infinite power is not being hesitant to use it when problems arise. Just because I suggest the Iremite Shan'iatu are diminished considerably doesn't mean they don't use sorcery to screw other nations, just that they prefer to handle it through mundane means first.<br /> <br /> Likewise, I've decided proper Necromancy with walking dead and all of that are something the Shan'iatu do not do casually. Once, they would have animated a nation and marched them around for centuries, but these days they very carefully select where they will spend the guttering sekhem which makes them more mortal with each passing year. The Shan'iatu CAN make an army of the dead crush a city, but they would far prefer to burn it with the comparatively renewable loyalty and blood of the well trained humans that serve them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T13:54:11-07:00","id":497852,"title":"","content":"The idea of a Shan'iatu 'Descent' would fit with some of the stuff in Book of the Deceived- they began to weaken after killing Azar and making a deal with the Devourer; which makes sense considering Ammut's purpose is to swallow Sekhem like a black hole- if my understanding is correct. But they were still far from weaklings. A fragment of a temakh, activated using Tier 3 of Blessed is the God-King by one of the Deceived, still has its Physical and Mental Attributes boosted to 10 along with their Occult Skill. That's not too shabby, and that's still just a <i>fragment</i> of one. Provided, that is, if I'm not misinterpreting it and using that power summons the temakh itself- in full -to inhabit that particular body. These are beings that could make even some of the more powerful Archmasters balk, or at the very least think twice about messing with them.<br /> <br /> In regards to the actual legions themselves, I'd look more closely at the Assyrians and the Romans. Three lines, most experienced in the back, youngest and most energetic in the front, using blocked rectangular units in a checkerboard position- this would allow them to rotate units out. The men would be armoured and armed in bronze- the more experienced and valuable troops in pyropus -with a fairly short (human-sized) spear as the primary weapon. I imagine the Maa-Kep Flail-Bearers would serve a purpose similar to the 'chosen men' of Roman centuries, a second-in-command to the unit officer who made sure everyone stayed in line and no one fled the battle by abandoning the rear ranks. They may or may not have had Sumerian-style 'war carts', four-wheeled vehicles of mayhem, but more likely they would have had the more advanced two-wheeled chariot.<br /> <br /> While the front lines of spearmen advanced on the enemy, the chariots would be acting as a smokescreen in front of them, concealing their movements and harrying the enemy with arrows. As charioteers were more elite warriors, typically nobility, richer citizens, or officers, this would enable them to change the approach of the army if need be. I imagine this is also where the appropriate Utterances and/or relics would come into play, if the general decided they were needed, to obliterate the enemy line from afar. As the infantry lines closed, the chariots would part to either side of the line to take up the flanks, and would presumably continue to harass the enemy flanks and attempt to get in the rear. They would either fire into the back of the enemy line, or more likely make a run for the enemy camp, raid their stores, and burn what they couldn't carry to deprive the enemy of resources. Iremite supply trains would likely have been highly organized, but given the powers of certain Utterances, if the Shan'iatu or Iremite Sekhem Sorcerers were willing to use magic, they might have required very little supplies, since there are Utterances that create food and water from virtually nothing, and they remain in place for years. This would have allowed them to easily make advance fortresses in attempts to conquest enemy territory. If, like Roman legions, they would try to fortify the camp with mobile barricades, part of which each soldier was carrying, then by going deep into enemy territory to strategic positions normally deemed uninhabitable, they could create a suitable base of operations by creating sources of water and oases within the walls, while the troops strengthen the walls with local resources.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T14:46:47-07:00","id":497899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=497852#post497852\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> These are beings that could make even some of the more powerful Archmasters balk, or at the very least think twice about messing with them.</div> </div> </div> </div>They are, their servants have Utterances that can obliterate Royal Avatars like it was nothing, as well as then barring that Archmaster from sticking their nose in the matters of the &quot;real world&quot; for a thousand years - well they cannot directly interact with the Fallen World through any means at least during that time.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T15:49:23-07:00","id":497947,"title":"","content":"Another thing to note with the legions is that we know exactly how many there were. 21 in total, each commanded by a pair of Shan'iatu Guildmasters. The Legions led by the seven Su-Menent appear to be the ones that were principally responsible for executing the war against Ki-En-Gir, according to the Book of the Deceived.<br /> <br /> Having double-checked the core book, Irem's legions are described as being &quot;vastly&quot; superior to those of the Ki-En-Gir. Without the help of their prophets, the war would have been a lot shorter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=497726#post497726\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Likewise, I've decided proper Necromancy with walking dead and all of that are something the Shan'iatu do not do casually. Once, they would have animated a nation and marched them around for centuries, but these days they very carefully select where they will spend the guttering sekhem which makes them more mortal with each passing year. The Shan'iatu CAN make an army of the dead crush a city, but they would far prefer to burn it with the comparatively renewable loyalty and blood of the well trained humans that serve them.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The section in the Book of the Deceived describing the war with Ki-En-Gir says that they animated a full third of all soldiers killed in that war. Although it similarly suggests that they used them as servants, not as additional warriors. This war took place well after their decline began.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T17:36:55-07:00","id":498031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=497947#post497947\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having double-checked the core book, Irem's legions are described as being &quot;vastly&quot; superior to those of the Ki-En-Gir. Without the help of their prophets, the war would have been a lot shorter.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;These Ki-En-Gir command the only professional soldiers the Iremites have seen besides themselves. The foreign warlords keep seers by their side, and although their troops are no match for the legions in open battle, they arrive at a stalemate: the oracles\u2019 foresight counters Irem\u2019s superior weaponry. Only the corpse-bearers declare victory, sending heavy caravans back to the City of Pillars.&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't get the same thing from that quote. It more seems like the Ki-En-Gir were dominating the invaders, at least until the necromancers got involved.<br /> <br /> My own fanon interpretation is that necromancy turned the tide of battle not through whole-scale animation of enemy armies, but by animation of enemy artists, thinkers and generals. In a way, similar to how the Persians later pilfer Egypt for artistic talent. In any rate, once these 'traitors' started serving Irem on a large scale the tide was turned and Uruk fell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T17:45:03-07:00","id":498040,"title":"","content":"Read it yourself. Ki-En-Gir troops &quot;are no match for the legions in open battle&quot;. It's pretty explicit that in a straight-up fight, Irem wins every time against even their greatest contemporary enemy.<br /> <br /> And I would hardly call something referred to as a &quot;stalemate&quot; as &quot;Ki-En-Gir were dominating the invaders&quot;. It sounds far more likely that they were winning and losing in equal measure, winning what they do only due to the ability to accurately predict the future.<br /> <br /> And the Book of the Deceived seems reasonably definitive as well on how it all ended. The Su-Menent Guildmasters began leading the legions in person. When they hit Ubar, they tortured and desecrated the body of the King of Ki-En-Gir. Ammut sent Her herald to possess his body, and at the end of the meeting the entire fortress was consumed. The Shan'iatu escaped, claimed it was their doing to their subjects (privately, the Deceived Shan'iatu at least were never sure whether it was Ammut's personal influence or the powers of their brothers and sisters in the Su-Menent), and after the fall of Ubar Ki-En-Gir crumbled. Their sorcerers were taken back for experimentation to see how their magic worked and expand Irem's occult knowledge.<br /> <br /> After that, Irem never faced another challenger. The focus of the empire turned towards the Rite of Return, and Irem fell without ever engaging in another campaign of conquest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T18:29:50-07:00","id":498068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=498040#post498040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Read it yourself. Ki-En-Gir troops &quot;are no match for the legions in open battle&quot;. It's pretty explicit that in a straight-up fight, Irem wins every time against even their greatest contemporary enemy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Many soldiers would probably have similar complains about mounted archery.<br /> <br /> Many dead soldiers.<br /> <br /> War is often about denying the opportunity for a 'straight-up fight', and when legitimate oracular ability is concerned, well...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T18:38:50-07:00","id":498080,"title":"","content":"And yet the situation is described as a stalemate, not a &quot;Ki-En-Gir walked all over Irem&quot;. The heavy casualties described could include as many Ki-En-Gir men as Iremite slave-soldiers, Irem's corpse-bearers were not exactly discriminatory in who they took back to the temples.<br /> <br /> And the situation is very different from mounted archers. Mounted archers can shoot a man with a sword without putting themselves in any serious risk. Using oracular abilities to determine where an enemy is going to be might let you spring an ambush, but at some point you are going to have to pit copper or even stone blades against armour of magic bronze. We don't know how precise or exact the oracular powers were, but by the description of the war we know that they weren't enough to let Ki-En-Gir win. Just stalemates. And they certainly weren't enough to stop the Su-Menent Guildmasters bulldozing down the front gates of their greatest fortress and torturing their king so badly he turned into the meat puppet of the Devourer of All Things.<br /> <br /> Ki-En-Gir as the superior military power in any respect is not supported by the documents we have to hand, basically.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T18:48:58-07:00","id":498087,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=498080#post498080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the situation is very different from mounted archers. Mounted archers can shoot a man with a sword without putting themselves in any serious risk. Using oracular abilities to determine where an enemy is going to be might let you spring an ambush, but at some point you are going to have to pit copper or even stone blades against armour of magic bronze.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not at all true. Armies need supply, and many soldiers have died without every having the opportunity to lift a blade or shoot an arrow, much to the frustration of the generals who invested considerable effort in training them. It's not a foregone conclusion that a fair fight where the enemy gets to use Bronze is needed to win the war.<br /> <br /> Also, a stalemate is great for a defending nation... not so great for an invading force.<br /> <br /> Irem obviously eventually won the war, but first it suffered humbling defeats by underestimating the Sumerian kings. In my opinion this was not a simple conflict like those that came before. It involved sacrifice, evolving tactical understanding, and multiple points where things could have gone wrong either way. It was as close to a world war as the period was capable of providing.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T19:00:32-07:00","id":498096,"title":"","content":"A stalemate with the massive casualties described both in Mummy and the Book of the Deceived isn't good for anyone, really. And if Ki-En-Gir was thrashing Irem, cutting off their supplies and starving entire armies to death without lifting a blade, I would use a very different word to the word &quot;stalemate&quot; to describe how the war was panning out.<br /> <br /> The section of the Book of the Deceived called &quot;Ammut's Second Lesson&quot; has a second account of the war to compliment that in the core book. It doesn't sound like much in the way of starvation is taking place. Bloody battles with many deaths on each side appear to have been the norm. Not only that, but the word used to describe massive casualties (both theirs and the enemies) from the perspective of the Shan'iatu was &quot;reward&quot;. I think when your opponent considers the loss of their own men a gift, fighting to a bloody draw couldn't remotely be called a win.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T21:15:51-07:00","id":498258,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=498096#post498096\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A stalemate with the massive casualties described both in Mummy and the Book of the Deceived isn't good for anyone, really. And if Ki-En-Gir was thrashing Irem, cutting off their supplies and starving entire armies to death without lifting a blade, I would use a very different word to the word &quot;stalemate&quot; to describe how the war was panning out.<br /> <br /> The section of the Book of the Deceived called &quot;Ammut's Second Lesson&quot; has a second account of the war to compliment that in the core book. It doesn't sound like much in the way of starvation is taking place. Bloody battles with many deaths on each side appear to have been the norm. Not only that, but the word used to describe massive casualties (both theirs and the enemies) from the perspective of the Shan'iatu was &quot;reward&quot;. I think when your opponent considers the loss of their own men a gift, fighting to a bloody draw couldn't remotely be called a win.</div> </div> </div> </div>It should be known that the section is written from the point of an unreliable narrator who's drive until now was to seek revenge upon Irem.<br /> <br /> Which given the amount of inhuman hate that the temakh seem to give, The one who wrote it was very introspective which I find delightfully odd.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T21:53:48-07:00","id":498288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=498258#post498258\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It should be known that the section is written from the point of an unreliable narrator who's drive until now was to seek revenge upon Irem.<br /> <br /> Which given the amount of inhuman hate that the temakh seem to give, The one who wrote it was very introspective which I find delightfully odd.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, the section we have from the Mummy core is written in terms of how the Arisen saw their own past. It's all ultimately suspect, but it's also all we have to work with.<br /> <br /> That said, the Temakh who wrote the section of the Book of the Deceived has more of a grudge to bear against the Su-Menent Shan'iatu leading <i>Irem's</i> armies, not to misrepresent the military effectiveness of a long-dead mortal culture. Ki-En-Gir was just a sacrifice so that the Su-Menent could contact Ammut and discover the Rite, never an enemy the Temakh had any particular grudge against. In terms of misrepresentation, the only charge I could think would be logical to level at the narrator is that he/it may have omitted information of little interest to himself. Now, on the one hand, it's entirely possible that the death of a lot of his own armies could have interested him. On the other hand, had Ki-En-Gir ever come close to &quot;humbling&quot; Irem, to use Piff's term, I would have thought their inclusion in the story would be presented differently than simply collateral damage in the Shan'iatu's quest for godhood.<br /> <br /> The constant challenging of information presented in the Book of the Deceived does wear on me a little. Yes, it's written using the unreliable narrator technique. That doesn't mean you need to caveat everything that comes from the book with &quot;but maybe that was just a lie&quot;. Presumably, from a meta perspective, if it was written in the book it serves a purpose in building the setting. If there are clues to the unreliable nature of the information, then that conflicting evidence builds the character of the narrator through seeing what was changed, and what it was changed into. If there's no conflicting evidence and no reasonable reason for the narrator to be lying, then to assume that information is inaccurate is to assume that the author of the section was churning out text with the deliberate goal of making the reader think that the setting is something other than how all evidence presents it. There will always be the smallest of question marks over everything written in that section, and the caveat that the World of Darkness is always what the GM wants it to be does, of course, apply, but challenging this kind of detail seems to presume a deliberate intent on behalf of the writers to deceive (heh) players as to the nature of the very setting in which they will be playing, without presenting any information that would reasonably make them aware of the fact that they <i>have</i> been lied to. To seriously believe that pointing out the unreliable nature of the narrator adds anything to the conversation under those circumstances is akin to adopting a view of the world with writers laughing in a smoky room because players were dumb enough to believe something they wrote.<br /> <br /> *whew* Sorry, needed to get that one off my chest.<br /> <br /> I'm personally semi-convinced that the narrator in question is the Poet. Possibly the Philosopher. The writing style doesn't quite seem to fit what we know of the others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T11:07:37-07:00","id":501911,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/495992-the-armies-of-irem?p=498288#post498288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm personally semi-convinced that the narrator in question is the Poet. Possibly the Philosopher. The writing style doesn't quite seem to fit what we know of the others.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could be the Philosopher due to the need to deconstruct everything and in this case, it would be himself but yes, I think the Poet would be a better fit.<br /> <br /> It is refreshing to see that some of the Temakhs having a bit of a 2d personality to them.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":604,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","user":"Shock","date":". 05-03-2015, 02:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T13:17:13-07:00","id":502077,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, a stalemate is great for a defending nation... not so great for an invading force. </div> </div> </div>It's really not. Pre-modern armies generally functioned by being constantly at war. It keeps them from being a drain on the home territory, while also keeping the military busy and uninvolved in internal politics. There's little advantage to be gained from having an enemy army rampaging around your land destroying everything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-03T13:26:07-07:00","id":502086,"title":"","content":"I was gonna say, go tell France the Western Front was a great thing for them <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}